## 8. Weak points: the questions most likely to hurt

Give these answers calmly and first. Admitting a limitation clearly is better than being caught out.

- **Single household for electricity.** One home is far noisier than a feeder or a city, which is why the MAPE is {{m:electricity.d1.test.SEL.mape|.0f}} percent. The method is the same for aggregated data, and the literature shows that aggregated load is much easier to forecast [@kong2019].
- **Simulated network for leaks.** L-Town is a benchmark network, not a real one. It is the standard open benchmark with exact leak start times. Real networks are noisier, so these detection results are a first test.
- **Observed weather used as a forecast.** I used the weather that actually happened, so real accuracy would be slightly lower. The ablation shows that weather changed next-day errors very little, so the effect is small.
- **Illustrative tariff and tank.** The cost savings show the mechanism, not a real utility's savings. A utility would plug in its own tariff and tank data, and the comparison against perfect foresight shows how much room remains.
- **Deep learning did not win everywhere.**
  - It won for volatile household load.
  - For water next-day forecasts, XGBoost beat the GRU ({{m:water.d1.test.xgboost.mae|.3f}} against {{m:water.d1.test.gru.mae|.3f}} L/s), because smooth district demand is explained well by lags and the calendar, and there were fewer than two years of training data.
  - For next-week totals, the selected models were {{m:electricity.w1.test.SEL.skill|.0%}} (electricity) and {{m:water.w1.test.SEL.skill|.0%}} (water) better than the baseline, but neither difference was significant ({{m:electricity.w1.significance_vs_naive.p_value|p}} and {{m:water.w1.significance_vs_naive.p_value|p}}).
- **European rather than Indian data.** There is no open Indian utility data at this resolution. The pipeline is data-agnostic, and Indian smart meters are being installed at scale.
- **Points excluded from MAPE.** For the electricity test set, {{m:electricity.d1.test.SEL.mape_excluded_points}} points were excluded; for water, {{m:water.d1.test.SEL.mape_excluded_points}}. MAE, which excludes nothing, is the main metric.
- **Leak precision near chance, and electricity false alarms.** Say this openly (Q19). A utility would use the alarms as a prompt to check, not as an automatic trigger.
- **Electricity peak-day alerts were weak.** With a threshold calibrated on validation forecasts, {{o:electricity_peak_alerts.test_calibrated.alert_days}} alerts caught {{o:electricity_peak_alerts.test_calibrated.hits}} of {{o:electricity_peak_alerts.test_calibrated.actual_peak_days}} peak days. Forecasts are smoother than actual peaks, so peak alerts need a dedicated model.

## 9. Papers to know

Read these five papers yourself before the viva; the summaries below are a revision aid, not a substitute. They are the papers cited most often in the report.

**Wang et al., review of smart-meter data analytics [@wang2019smartmeter].**
- **Problem:** how to use the huge volume of fine-grained consumption data from smart meters to make the power grid more efficient and sustainable.
- **Method:** an application-oriented review, organised by descriptive, predictive and prescriptive analytics. The main application areas it identifies are load analysis, load forecasting and load management, and it reviews the techniques used for each.
- **Data:** the published literature.
- **Findings:** open research directions, including big-data issues, new machine-learning methods, new business models, the energy transition, and data privacy and security.
- **Why it matters to you:** it frames what smart-meter data is used for, and supports your answer on privacy.

**Antunes et al., short-term water demand forecasting with machine learning [@antunes2018].**
- **Problem:** many water utilities run their pumps on the immediate demand: pumps start at a minimum tank level and stop at a maximum. Planning on forecast demand instead allows pumping when electricity is cheaper.
- **Method:** neural networks, random forests, support vector machines and k-nearest neighbours, plus a weighted combination of them. They also study the effect of weather, seasonality, the amount of training data and the forecast window.
- **Data:** real data from two Portuguese water utilities, with results compared against ARIMA benchmarks.
- **Why it matters to you:** it is the basis of your water-energy argument and of the level-triggered rule you compared against.

**Vrachimis et al., the BattLeDIM competition [@vrachimis2022battledim].**
- **Problem:** methods for detecting and locating leaks in water networks are hard to compare without common benchmarks.
- **Method:** a competition in which teams from academia and industry detected and located leaks. They used time-series analysis, statistics, machine learning, mathematical programming, metaheuristics and engineering judgement, and were scored with realistic economic criteria.
- **Data:** flow and pressure readings from sensors in a virtual network, L-Town.
- **Main finding:** leak detection and isolation methods still need further development, and more open benchmarks are needed.
- **Why it matters to you:** it is the source of your leak labels.

**Alvisi et al., the Battle of Water Demand Forecasting [@alvisi2025bwdf].**
- **Problem:** comparing methods for short-term urban water demand forecasting.
- **Method:** an international competition held with the WDSA-CCWI conference in Ferrara, in which teams from around the world forecast demand in a set of real district metered areas.
- **Main findings:** integrated forecasting approaches matter, and methods should be judged on more than one metric, case study and period.
- **Why it matters to you:** it is the source of your water data, and it is why you checked robustness across districts and report several metrics.

**Kong et al., LSTM for residential load forecasting [@kong2019].**
- **Problem:** forecasting the load of a single household is much harder than forecasting aggregated load, because individual use is highly volatile and uncertain.
- **Method:** a framework based on an LSTM recurrent neural network.
- **Data:** publicly available real residential smart-meter data, compared against a range of benchmarks, including state-of-the-art methods.
- **Main finding:** the LSTM outperformed the competing methods for short-term load forecasting of individual households.
- **Why it matters to you:** it supports your finding that a recurrent network (your GRU) was best for household electricity.

**Also read (recommended in STUDENT_REVIEW.md):**
- **Marino et al. [@marino2016]** compared a standard LSTM with an LSTM sequence-to-sequence model on electricity data from one residential customer, at one-minute and one-hour resolution. The standard LSTM failed on the minute data but worked well on the hourly data, while the sequence-to-sequence model worked on both. This supports your choice to average to hourly values.
- **Guo et al. [@guo2018]** developed a GRU network for short-term water demand forecasting, with a correction module for accumulated error. It outperformed a conventional neural network and SARIMA. Your water result went the other way (XGBoost beat the GRU), and Q1, Q13 and the weak-points section explain why.
